<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Hedmark</h1><p class="st-meta">750 CE – 880 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Inland kingdom around Lake Mjøsa, one of the core lands of Halfdan the Black, where petty kings survived until Olaf Haraldsson.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hedmark, the farming land on the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mjosa">Lake Mjøsa</a>, was one of the richest districts of inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. The sagas say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halfdan-the-black">Halfdan the Black of Vestfold</a> gained it by war and inheritance in the 9th century, making it part of the base from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> built his kingdom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hedmark's wealth came from grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and iron from the surrounding forests. It shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsivating">Eidsivating</a> law assembly with the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upplands-norway">Uppland</a> districts. Local kings kept their position under the high kings, and the sagas name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrorek">Hrørek</a> as king of Hedmark in the early 11th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a> crushed a revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upplands-norway">Uppland</a> kings and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrorek">Hrørek</a> blinded, ending the line of local kings. Hedmark became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamar">Hamar</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mjosa">Lake Mjøsa</a>, became the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1152.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hedmark.jpg" alt="Hedmark" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Cato Edvardsen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atnsj%C3%B8en_og_Rondane_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>750 CE – 880 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Heiðmǫrk, Kingdom of Hedmark</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=815&amp;focus=hedmark" aria-label="Hedmark on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4922 147 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hedmark lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5242 403L5225 425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5242 403h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5242 403h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=815&amp;focus=hedmark">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
